1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Fortunately, on Earth, people can adapt to this situation and live without certain amenities

2
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
as generations did before them.

3
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
In space, however, lack of power is not just a temporary inconvenience, but a grim denial

4
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
of survival.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
For our astronauts depend upon sensitive equipment, systems of high sophistication, bound and

6
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
ruled by one single matter of life and death.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:06,000
need for energy, power, continuous and uninterrupted, for days, months, or better still, years.

8
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
True solar cells can operate some unmanned missions and fuel cells can take us briefly

9
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
to the moon, but the moon is near at hand compared to other planets in our solar system.

10
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Close or far in space, man must take his earth environment along, and if you can't take it

11
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
with you. You just don't go. Of course simulating the Earth's environment is by no means a new

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
idea. Crews of nuclear submarines live in health and comfort for months at a time, submerged

13
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
in an alien and hostile environment. This is possible because their nuclear power system

14
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,000
operates continuously for long periods of time. Space voyagers, too, need this kind

15
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,000
of power. And that's where SNAP-8 comes in, a program to study the technology of converting

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
nuclear energy into useful electricity for space exploration. The present SNAP-8 system

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
is designed to operate for over a year, providing sufficient power to meet the needs of 125

18
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
earthbound Americans. In space, this means oxygen, drinking water, air conditioning,

19
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:18,000
lighting, and communication. In short, SNAP-8 is providing the technology for the maintenance

20
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
of equipment and sustenance of light. In the SNAP-8 system, an atomic reactor supplies

21
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
heat, which is transformed into electricity by the power conversion system. Some of the

22
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,000
principle SNAP-8 components are a boiler, condenser, turbine and associated alternator,

23
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:46,000
a container holding electrical controls, expansion reservoirs for the various system loops and

24
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
pumps. Heat from the nuclear reactor is absorbed by a liquid alloy called NaK, which is composed

25
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
of sodium and potassium. While passing through the reactor, the alloy is heated to about

26
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:02,000
1300 degrees Fahrenheit. This heat is transferred to a boiler before the NaK returns to the

27
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
reactor to be reheated. This is the first of several interrelated loops which make up

28
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:14,000
the SNAP-8 electrical power system. Of course, future developments could employ another nuclear

29
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
heat source, radioisotopes, in place of the reactor. The second loop is filled with liquid

30
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,000
mercury, which is vaporized in the boiler by the heat released by NaK loop number one.

31
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
These mercury vapors drive the turbine alternator, producing electrical power. After driving

32
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
the turbine, the vapors are recondensed into a liquid. The liquid then returns from the

33
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
condenser to the boiler, completing loop number two. Because NaK is an effective heat absorber

34
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
and coolant, it is used in a third loop to carry the excess heat given off by the mercury

35
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
to a radiator, where it is dissipated into space. A fourth loop, filled with oil, is

36
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
provided to cool the components and lubricate the bearings. This combination of closed loops

37
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
permits materials to be used over and over again, making such power-producing systems

38
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
capable of continuous operation for months and even years.

39
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Development of the SNAP-8 electrical power conversion system is now in progress at Aerojet General's von Karman Center

40
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
under direction of the National Aeronautics and Space Administration.

41
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Simultaneously, development of the nuclear reactor

42
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
is underway at Atomics International for the Atomic Energy Commission.

43
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
The Aerojet test complex is the center of all non-nuclear SNAP-8 testing activities.

44
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Here, massive heaters supply the thermal energy,

45
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
which in space will be supplied by a compact nuclear reactor.

46
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Each heater brings NaK liquid alloy to high temperatures,

47
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
while pumps and pipes transport it to the test bays where it is used to test SNAP-8 components.

48
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
The complex is a maze of closed loops and sealed parts.

49
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Only instrument readings indicate that tests are going on,

50
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
all different kinds, such as the recently completed 3,000-hour hot test of the NaK pump shown here.

51
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:08,000
For not only must designs be evaluated and improved, but the materials themselves must

52
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
be studied, as they are subjected to high temperatures, low pressures, and radiation

53
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
for months on end.

54
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
The components for these and future tests are fabricated and assembled in special dust-free

55
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:26,000
clean rooms, for even a little dust can cause parts to fail.

56
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Thus, SNAP8 is not a paper study but a very important advanced technology program based

57
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
on well-established principles.

58
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Here, consoles monitor SNAP8 test hardware during development tests and endurance runs.

59
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Of course, this is an experimental system, an earthbound, greatly enlarged version of

60
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,000
a possible future flight system on which arrangements and functions of components can be studied

61
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
as they work together producing SNAP8 power.

62
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Today, successful proof testing of individual components has led to a progressive buildup

63
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:07,000
of a complete power conversion unit, a necessary step before integrated testing with the reactor.

64
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
This power conversion system buildup is proceeding in four major steps. Checkout of boiler, condenser

65
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:22,000
and mercury pump, addition of the turbine alternator, NaK pumps, and finally, after

66
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,000
startup equipment is checked out, a 10,000-hour endurance test of the entire system. Only by such

67
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:34,000
exhaustive testing can we thoroughly understand a new system and its capabilities. For instance,

68
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
development of the nuclear submarine, which later became the indispensable platform for the Polaris

69
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:46,000
missile, was started immediately after the war, but it wasn't developed overnight. In fact, it took

70
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:53,000
nine years, nine years, accepted today as time well spent.

71
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Snap-8 technology needs this same kind of long lead time. For when a system must be

72
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:10,000
built to operate for years, its basic concept and the actual hardware must be proven by

73
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:17,000
years of testing. And when lives of men are at stake, one dares not and does not take

74
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:24,000
risks. SNAP-8 is the only major technology program of its kind conducted today. Obviously,

75
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:32,000
future space missions and their associated power supplies must be planned years in advance.

76
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:39,000
The knowledge gained from the SNAP-8 program will allow them to become realities. The desirable

77
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,000
The desirable features of nuclear systems make them candidates for many kinds of missions.

78
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:56,000
One of the earliest applications might be for TV satellites capable of broadcasting educational and informational programs directly to homes all over the world.

79
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:03,000
Large orbiting space stations could use nuclear electric power to support earth observation and space research

80
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,000
or make them useful springboards for deep space exploration.

81
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Permanent bases on the Moon would also require large quantities of power to bore into and thoroughly explore the lunar surface.

82
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:19,000
And as we continue to reach farther and farther into space,

83
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,000
requirements for life-sustaining and equipment-maintaining power will become progressively heavier.

84
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
All this may be nearer than we think.

85
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Ten years ago, the Moon looked very far away,

86
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:36,000
and yet today it is not only a realistic goal, but the focal point of our national space effort.

87
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:43,000
And just as our present objectives exceed those of a decade ago, so our goals for 1975

88
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
are likely to exceed any predictions we make now.

89
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:53,000
But whatever our goals, we will need the technology being built today in the SNAP-8 program.

90
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:59,000
For without it, our manned explorations could remain confined to very limited horizons.

91
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:04,000
If space is this century's frontier, we must recognize that compared to the great voyagers

92
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:11,000
of the past we are barely out of port but to travel beyond our earth and its companion

93
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:16,000
satellite to other planets in our solar family we must have a continuous source of substantial

94
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:25,000
electrical power for months or better still years snappy technology can supply this power
